From: atomchild411 <143453386+atomchild411@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 23:10:00 -0900
Subject: [PATCH] WTF: IRIX RAM size counted in 64 bits, capped for n32

On an Octane with 8 GB, WebKit's network process killed itself on the
first big page ("Unable to shrink memory footprint of process (32 MB)
below the kill thresold (0 MB). Killed"), and every load stalled after
it. ramSize() multiplied the page count by the page size in an n32
size_t (32 bits): 8 GB wrapped to 0, and every memory-kill threshold is
at most 90% of it. With 2 GB it fit.

The product is now taken in 64 bits and, where size_t is narrower,
capped at 2 GB, the address space an n32 process has.

Applies after 0001.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/Source/WTF/wtf/RAMSize.cpp
+++ b/Source/WTF/wtf/RAMSize.cpp
@@ -72,7 +72,13 @@
     struct rminfo info { };
     if (sysmp(MP_SAGET, MPSA_RMINFO, &info, sizeof(info)) < 0)
         return 512 * MB;
-    return static_cast<size_t>(info.physmem) * getpagesize();
+    // Counted in 64 bits and capped at what this process can address: an
+    // n32 size_t is 32 bits, and 8 GB (an Octane's maximum) wrapped to 0,
+    // which made every memory-kill threshold 0 MB.
+    uint64_t bytes = static_cast<uint64_t>(info.physmem) * static_cast<uint64_t>(getpagesize());
+    if constexpr (sizeof(size_t) < sizeof(uint64_t))
+        bytes = std::min<uint64_t>(bytes, 2 * static_cast<uint64_t>(GB));
+    return static_cast<size_t>(bytes);
 #elif OS(UNIX) || OS(HAIKU)
     long pages = sysconf(_SC_PHYS_PAGES);
     long pageSize = sysconf(_SC_PAGE_SIZE);
